  "facts": [
    "Opened on 4 September 1964, the Forth Road Bridge became the longest suspension bridge outside the United States at that time, spanning 1,700 metres across the Firth of Forth.",
    "Construction began in September 1958, employing 210,000 tons of concrete and 9 miles of dual‑carriageway approaches, and cost £15.1 million, a record for a European steel suspension bridge.",
    "In 2003 an inspection revealed an 8‑10% loss of strength in the main cables, prompting traffic restrictions by 2014 and a 2018 refurbishment that re‑designated the bridge as a public‑transport corridor."
